!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/39/m/meta.tn?sha256=0335e318a8ea9b3a9e108ac6b0ef6cf930def8353ce3cafbb241d4c037cacd54"
@meta.doc:"""
  TSON meta-schema — the kernel, pre-loaded (2026 Revision 39 draft).

  Every declaration of the series' type system sits under one entry, `meta`,
  a schema map nested in this document's root: the structural kinds and
  constructors, the atom constructors the core type library (core.tn)
  instantiates, and the annotation types. A schema whose import closure holds
  this document reserves one name of it, `meta`, and names each entry by its
  path from the root, `meta.record`, `meta.integer_type` ([TSON-SCHEMA]
  §2.2.3, §5.12). Every name resolves from the root, so an entry here naming
  a sibling writes it by its path, as an importer does.

  The document imports nothing and is never resolved from a document: it is
  pre-loaded by special resolution ([TSON-SCHEMA] §3.4), and must be the
  revision the processor was built for. Its root tag and the tag of the map
  it nests, `!schema`, name its own `meta.schema`, as every schema document's
  root tag does. Implementations ship its resolved structure, and this
  document describes it. User schemas normally reach it through core.tn,
  which imports it and names the common instances at its own root.

  Hash pins are real digests: sha256 over every byte of a document past its
  `!!id` line ([TSON-DATA] §2.2.1), which is what lets an `!!id` carry the
  digest of the document it identifies. Core and net pin this file, so an
  edit here moves every pin above it.

  Beside the structural kinds and constructors, three families of
  declarations live here:

  1. Structural constructors: `bytes_type` (with the `bytes_encoding` enum
     selecting its text spelling), whose instance in core is `bytes`.
     Fixed-size arrays have no constructor of their own: `[T; N]` is the
     spelling.

  2. The `set` template — the `set<T>` spelling of the `set_type`
     constructor.

  3. Constraint vocabulary constructors for the atom families core
     instantiates: numeric (`float_type` for the approximate tier, with the
     `ieee_format` enum; `decimal_type` for the exact tier; `rational_type`;
     `complex_type`, with the `complex_component` enum), temporal
     (`date_type`, `time_type`, `datetime_type`, `duration_type`,
     `period_type`), identifier (`uuid_type`, `uri_type`), and text
     (`email_type`). The network constructors are net.tn's, under `net`.

  Plus annotation types — the classifying markers `ordering`, `bounded`,
  `exact`, `numeric`; the checked assertion `disjoint`; and the documentary
  `deprecated`, `title`, `comment`, `examples`, `read_only`, `write_only`. An
  annotation resolves in the annotating schema's namespace ([TSON-SCHEMA]
  §3.3.3, §6), so these reach every schema whose closure includes this
  document, as `@meta.title`, and as `@title` where core.tn names them at its
  root.

  An annotation never changes a value, its type, or whether it is valid:
  validation is over values, and a schema with every annotation erased admits
  exactly the same values. [TSON-SCHEMA] §6 admits two further things within
  that rule — a load-time check, and a directive over how a class of encodings
  represents a value — and the annotations declared here use the first alone.
  The same stance governs the type docs: a value space is described without
  reference to any encoding, and where a doc mentions how TSON text spells a
  value it says so by name.

  Constructors that bind to a governing specification compose with
  `atom_specification` and pin the spec field with `=`. Their instances in
  core and net are empty, `float_type` aside, whose instances select a
  `format`.

  Constraint fields whose values are of an atom whose instance this document
  does not declare — a bound of the constrained atom's own family — use the
  `value` escape hatch; the resolver reads each such token under the atom it
  stands for once that atom is in scope. A field that counts something — a
  length, a digit count, a prefix length, a precision — is typed by
  `non_negative_integer`, and one that may be negative by `integer`.

  The ordered numeric families state their bounds as field groups
  ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.11): one inclusive-or-exclusive group per side, so an
  inclusive and an exclusive bound on the same side is unrepresentable.
  Value-level coherence (the lower bound not exceeding the upper) is a
  schema-load check.

  `multiple_of`, wherever it appears (`decimal_type`, `rational_type`,
  `duration_type`, `period_type`, and `integer_type`), follows one rule: the
  step is strictly positive — zero or a negative step is a schema-load error —
  the test ignores the sign of the value, a refinement may only tighten the
  step to an integer multiple of the inherited one, and the step composes with
  every other facet present. `members`, where it appears, is likewise uniform
  in what it admits: every member satisfies the other facets on the same body
  or the schema fails to load. Refinement differs by tier — the numeric tiers
  shrink the set, `text_type` sets it once.
  """
!schema {
  @meta.doc:"""
    The series' type system: every kind, constructor, base instance and
    annotation type, each named by its path, `meta.record`.
    """
  meta => !schema {
    @meta.doc:"""
      The structural root: every constructor IS-A `top`. Every constructor
      transitively IS-A `atom`, `product` or `sum`, except `alias` and
      `template`, which compose with `top` directly; `atom`, `product` and `sum`
      IS-A `type`, which IS-A `top`. Construction transfers kind, not IS-A, so
      instances and fresh records sit under the kinds with empty chains. `top`
      and the kinds beneath it are abstract: none has instances of its own, so a
      direct application of one is refused as any abstract record's is, naming
      the subtypes that would satisfy it.

      A schema map's entries are types or values (`schema`). An entry whose
      value is built by a constructor IS-A `type` is a type; one whose value is
      built by `alias` or `template` is an alias or an open entry; and one whose
      value is an instance of an ordinary type, `orders => !api { ... }` or
      `create_order => !operation { ... }`, is a value, marked by nothing but
      its tag.
      """
    top       => abstract {}

    @meta.doc:"""
      Where an entry sits in the IS-A order. `parents` is what was written: the
      parents of a record template, one of which may be an application
      (`<T> result<T> & { ... }`), kept on the template entry once they resolve;
      a closed instantiation keeps none. It is the only list that ever holds an
      application. `supertypes` is the closed chain: every entry this one IS-A,
      complete to a fixed point through applied parents ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.8),
      each a name without arguments, sorted, and empty for a fresh record, an
      instance and a subtracted record alike. `subtypes` is the inverse, over
      the entries of this map only ([TSON-SCHEMA] §3.3.4).
      """
    type_chain => {
      parents?:    [meta.type_kind]
      supertypes?: [meta.type_kind]
      subtypes?:   [meta.type_kind]
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      The kind of kinds that describe the shape of a data value: `atom`,
      `product` and `sum` IS-A `type`, and nothing else does. An entry whose
      value is a body whose constructor IS-A `type` is a type; an `alias`
      or a `template` is not, composing `top`, and an entry whose value is an
      instance of an ordinary type is a value, never a type.

      A type carries its IS-A chain. A declaration never writes `chain`, and a
      constructor never declares a field of that name: the resolver computes the
      chain from composition and refinement and the linker inverts it. On ingest
      it is read as stated and never recomputed, because a flat body and its
      chain cannot be recomputed from each other.
      """
    type      => abstract meta.top & {
      chain?: meta.type_chain
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      The kind of a scalar: a value written as one token, which its atom family
      reads into a value.
      """
    atom      => abstract meta.type & {}

    @meta.doc:"""
      The kind of a compound value of parts: a record, an array, a map or a
      tuple. `access_pattern` says whether a part is reached by position or by
      name, and `size_type` whether the number of parts is fixed by the type.
      """
    product   => abstract meta.type & {
      access_pattern: meta.product_access_type
      size_type:      meta.product_size_type
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      The kind of a value that is one of several types: a choice or a scope.
      """
    sum       => abstract meta.type & {}

    @meta.doc:"""
      A map keyed by names: the constructor of a namespace type, whose values
      are maps from `key_type` to `value_type`, `ordered` as a map's are. A
      namespace type is an instance, as a map type is one of `map`:
      `api => !meta.namespace { key_type: identifier  value_type: method }`, and
      an entry holding a value of it, `orders => !api { ... }`, is an ordinary
      value of the type `api`. Its keys are names, of an identifier family,
      which is what a qualified name's segment is, and a segment selects a key
      of a namespace value and of no other map.

      `key_type` is the family every key is read and judged under. Absent, the
      host of the value supplies it: a record's `fields` are keyed by the
      record's `name_type`. A value is never void and has no size bounds.
      The schema map (`schema`) and a record's fields (`field_map`) are the
      kernel's two.
      """
    namespace => meta.product & {
      access_pattern?: meta.product_access_type = NAMED
      size_type?:      meta.product_size_type = VARIABLE
      key_type?:       meta.identifier_kind
      value_type:      meta.type_kind
      ordered?:        meta.boolean ~ false
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      Aliasing body — the entry `target` names: a type, or a constructor, since
      an alias of a constructor is applied as its target is ([TSON-SCHEMA]
      §3.3.1), a constructor's value being a record. A partial application
      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.10, `uuid_pair => <B> pair<uuid, B>`) is an open entry,
      and its target is the reference as written, inside the
      `template.template` text: `!meta.alias { target: pair<uuid B> }`.

      An alias is a hop, not a rewrite: resolved output states the chain as the
      author wrote it, and a use site names whatever entry it names. A processor
      collapses the chain when it compiles readers, after linking, once per
      entry. A walk stops at an argument-bearing target, which is an application
      rather than a further hop.
      """
    alias => meta.top & {
      target: meta.type_kind
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      One parameter of an open entry: its name, and the type an argument for it
      is read as — the declared type of the slot it stands in, in the vocabulary
      of the constructor the held body applies. It is derived from the body and
      never required of the author: a type slot gives `type_kind`; a scalar slot
      of the applied constructor gives that slot's declared type (`min_items: N`
      gives `non_negative_integer`); a routed default or fixed value gives the
      field's own declared type (`w?: int32 ~ N` gives `int32`); and a parameter
      passed to another template takes that template's recorded type for the
      position, with `type_kind` where nothing grounds it. A parameter is a type
      parameter exactly when its `type` is `type_kind`. A `type` may name an
      earlier parameter of the same template, where a routed default's field is
      typed by one.

      A declaration may narrow what it derives by writing a type after the name,
      `<T: text, N: int8>`, read by the kind the positions give. On a value
      parameter it narrows `type` itself and must IS-A the derived type. On a
      type parameter it is `bound`: an argument must name a type that IS-A it.
      `bound` is present only where `type` is `type_kind`, and a parameter
      passed to another template inherits that template's bound for the
      position, so an unannotated parameter is bounded as narrowly as its uses
      require.
      """
    template_param => {
      name:   meta.param_name
      type:   meta.type_kind
      bound?: meta.type_kind
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      Held body — the body of an entry that declares type parameters and applies
      any constructor but `record` ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.10), a record template
      being a `record` with `parameters`. `template` is the constructor
      application as written, unread until materialisation substitutes the
      parameters away, and `parameters` lists what it binds, each a
      `template_param`.

      The text is how a held body is written in resolved output; a processor
      holds the parsed form, which this vocabulary cannot represent. A held body
      is not a value of this vocabulary: a parameter stands wherever a token
      stands — `min_items: N` as readily as `element_type: T` — so a body
      carrying one is not typed by any constructor's own record shape until it
      closes.

      An open entry's value is this, built by a constructor IS-A `top`. What is
      compared is the parsed form and never the text: an open
      synthetic's identity is derived from its held binding record, so two
      spellings of one form must reduce to one entry, and whitespace is free.

      Composes `top` alone: it is not a type, its open form denotes nothing, and
      naming it at a type position is an error. An application is its expansion,
      and carries whatever chain the expansion's own `^` or `&` gives it.
      """
    template => meta.top & {
      parameters: [meta.template_param]
      template:   meta.text
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      Escape hatch primitive. `value` is the unconstrained instance of
      value_type, an atom constructor with no constraint vocabulary: the token,
      uninterpreted, read by the type the position hands it to. A value-typed
      field is read by the declared type it stands for and never by base type
      resolution, which applies only in schemaless documents ([TSON-DATA] §4).
      value is not narrowable, and a value position is not a scope: it admits no
      directive.

      Used for record_field.value (the type of fixed/default values, which must
      be the field's declared type) and for constraint vocabulary fields whose
      constrained atom has no instance here (float_type.min, decimal_type.min,
      etc.).
      """
    value_type => meta.atom & {}

    value => !meta.value_type {}

    @meta.doc:"""
      Void primitive. The instance of void_type, an atom constructor with no
      constraint vocabulary whose parsing contract admits only the void sentinel
      `_`. It binds no host value. void is the target type for bare annotations
      (`@T` with no value); the resolver fills the implicit `_` and validates
      against this contract. See [TSON-SCHEMA] §6.
      """
    void_type => meta.atom & {}

    void => !meta.void_type {}

    @meta.doc:"The two-valued type of a constraint field that is a switch."
    boolean => !meta.enum [true false]

    @meta.doc:"""
      Fixed-width integer representation: a bit width paired with its
      two's-complement signedness.
      """
    integer_size => {
      bits:    meta.non_negative_integer
      signed:  meta.boolean
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      The members of an integer_type. Member identity is [TSON-DATA] §4.3's, so
      80 and 0x50 are one member and a duplicate rather than two.
      """
    integer_member_set => !meta.set_type { element_type: meta.integer  min_items: 1 }

    @meta.doc:"""
      Integer constraint vocabulary. Atom constructor whose instance,
      `meta.integer`, is the arbitrary-precision integer. Its field types name
      `meta.integer`, the constructor's own instance; a name at a type position
      orders nothing, so the mutual reference closes. Both reach every schema
      whose import closure includes this document.

      Bounds: each side is a field group ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.11) — the inclusive
      `min` or the exclusive `exclusive_min`; likewise `max` or `exclusive_max`
      — so at most one bound per side holds by shape. That the lower bound not
      exceed the upper (across whichever forms are present) is a schema-load
      coherence check.

      Fixed width: `size` gives a fixed representation width; when absent the
      integer is unbounded arbitrary precision (the base `integer`). Width and
      signedness derive the representable range — signed n-bit is [-2^(n-1),
      2^(n-1)-1], unsigned n-bit is [0, 2^n-1] — so `size` and the bound groups
      are equivalent ways to state a bound. The two compose: bounds and
      `multiple_of` narrow within the width-derived range, and a bound outside
      that range is a resolver error. Signedness is two's complement. `size`
      names the target representation a consumer decodes into, not the source
      form. Resolver output stores `size` as written and does not expand it to
      bounds.

      Step: `multiple_of` is strictly positive; zero or a negative value is a
      schema-load error. The test ignores the sign of the value, so -30 is a
      multiple of 15. A refinement may only tighten `multiple_of` to an integer
      multiple of the inherited value (15 under 5 is a tightening; 10 under 15
      is an error). It composes with every other facet present: a value must
      satisfy all of them.

      Enumeration: `members` lists the admitted values outright — the spelling
      for numeric codes, an enum's members being text
      (`!integer ^ { members: [1 2 3] }`, not `!meta.enum [1 2 3]`). Every
      member must itself satisfy the bounds, the width and `multiple_of` present
      on the same body — a member outside them is a schema-load error — and a
      refinement may only shrink the set (the member-set rule of the tightening
      table). A value must be a member and satisfy the other facets; with a
      coherent set the second test is implied by the first.
      """
    integer_type => meta.atom & {
      size?: meta.integer_size
      ( min: meta.integer | exclusive_min: meta.integer )?
      ( max: meta.integer | exclusive_max: meta.integer )?
      multiple_of?: meta.non_negative_integer
      members?:     meta.integer_member_set
    }

    integer => !meta.integer_type {}

    @meta.doc:"""
      Non-negative integer — the type of every facet that counts something:
      lengths, item counts, digit counts, bit widths, prefix lengths,
      fractional-second precision. That `bits` wants more than zero is a
      coherence check, not a type.
      """
    non_negative_integer => !meta.integer ^ { min: 0 }

    @meta.doc:"""
      The members of a text_type. The set's own uniqueness compares the text as
      written; each member is then put into the constrained type's normalization
      form, and two that are one value there are a duplicate, as text_type says.
      """
    text_member_set => !meta.set_type { element_type: meta.text  min_items: 1 }

    @meta.doc:"""
      Text constraint vocabulary. Atom constructor whose instance, `text`, is
      the kernel's Unicode code point sequence type. Lengths count code points.

      `members` lists the admitted strings outright. Every member must satisfy
      the other facets on the same body, the pattern included.

      `normalization` is the form a value is put into. A value is its token's
      text, unquoted, unescaped and then put into that form; every other facet
      judges the value and never the spelling, and so does every comparison of
      two values. A member is a value too, and two members that are one value
      are refused.

      `pattern` and `members` are each settable once: a refinement may set one
      the source left unset, or restate the source's own, and never change it.
      `normalization` is fixed where the type is constructed: a refinement
      restates it or leaves it, since changing it would change what a token
      means rather than narrow the values admitted.
      """
    text_type => meta.atom & {
      min_length?:    meta.non_negative_integer
      max_length?:    meta.non_negative_integer
      length?:        meta.non_negative_integer
      pattern?:       meta.regex
      members?:       meta.text_member_set
      normalization?: meta.normalization ~ NONE
    }

    text => !meta.text_type {}

    @meta.doc:"""
      Mixin record contributing the `spec` field to specification-bound atoms.
      """
    atom_specification => { spec: meta.iri }

    @meta.doc:"""
      The base a UAX #31 Start or Continue set is drawn from: XID_Start /
      XID_Continue, ID_Start / ID_Continue, their ASCII members (Start A-Z a-z,
      Continue adding 0-9 and "_"), or the empty set.
      """
    identifier_base => !meta.enum [XID ID ASCII NONE]

    @meta.doc:"""
      The form a text value is put into. NONE keeps the text as written. NFC and
      NFKC are Unicode's normalization forms. NFKC_CASEFOLD is toNFKC_Casefold,
      UAX #31's form for names compared without case: over ASCII it lowercases,
      so Content-Type and content-type are one value. ASCII_CASEFOLD maps A-Z to
      a-z and changes nothing else, the comparison of case-insensitive ASCII
      names such as URI schemes and HTTP field names.
      """
    normalization => !meta.enum [NONE NFC NFKC NFKC_CASEFOLD ASCII_CASEFOLD]

    @meta.doc:"""
      Identifier constraint vocabulary: a UAX #31 identifier profile, with the
      text facets applied inside it. It composes text_type's record shape, so it
      takes lengths, a pattern such as a naming convention, and members for a
      closed vocabulary of names. The profile is UAX #31's R1 default identifier
      syntax:

        identifier := Start Continue* (Medial Continue+)*
        Start      := (start ∪ start_add) − exclude
        Continue   := (continue ∪ continue_add) − exclude
        Medial     := medial

      where start and continue name the base each set is drawn from, and each of
      start_add, continue_add, medial and exclude is the set of code points its
      text holds. A medial character stands only between two others and never
      beside another medial, so medial must be disjoint from Start and Continue.
      The profile judges the value, which is the text put into the normalization
      form; the form defaults to NFC here. A join control (U+200C, U+200D) is
      admitted only in the contexts UTS #39 §3.1.1.1 permits, whatever the
      profile.

      The profile facets are fixed where the profile is constructed: a
      refinement restates each or leaves it, and narrows only the text facets. A
      different profile is a fresh !identifier_type.

      identifier is [TSON-DATA] §7.7's instance, and the type of every naming
      position in the series — type names, field names and parameter names,
      through the three roles below. A rule stated here reaches all of them. An
      identifier is a name, not a lexeme: it is the decoded text of a token,
      after unquoting, escape processing and normalisation, and it is
      constrained however it was spelled. Its profile is stated beside the
      unquoted-token profile of [TSON-DATA] §7.1:

        Start    = XID_Start
        Continue = XID_Continue + "-"

      and its value is the text in NFC. The profile is the token profile minus
      the extensions the number grammar requires, so an identifier never begins
      with a digit or a sign, which subsumes the rule that numbers are not
      declarable names. "+" is dropped from Continue, and "." is reserved as a
      future identifier separator. Everything else follows from XID membership:
      whitespace, control characters, emoji, unassigned code points and every
      format character but the two join controls are none of them XID_Continue.

      Every part of identifier's profile lies inside the unquoted-token profile,
      so every identifier is a well-formed unquoted token and none ever needs
      quoting. Another profile carries no such guarantee, and a value under it
      may need quoting.

      A schema that types a value or a map key as a name of another family
      declares its own identifier_type instance under a name of its own, such as
      `slug => !meta.identifier_type { continue_add: "-" }`.
      """
    identifier_type => meta.text_type & meta.atom_specification & {
      spec?:          = "https://www.unicode.org/reports/tr31/"
      start?:         meta.identifier_base ~ XID
      continue?:      meta.identifier_base ~ XID
      start_add?:     meta.text
      continue_add?:  meta.text
      medial?:        meta.text
      exclude?:       meta.text
      normalization?: meta.normalization ~ NFC
    }

    identifier => !meta.identifier_type { continue_add: "-" }

    @meta.doc:"""
      IRI constraint vocabulary per RFC 3987. Composes with the text_type
      constructor to inherit its record shape (length and pattern fields) plus
      the atom_specification mixin's spec field plus iri-specific fields.
      `schemes` lists the admitted schemes as scheme_name values, which fold
      case as RFC 3986 §3.1 compares a scheme, and a value's scheme is compared
      in that form; a value with no scheme is outside it. `allow_relative`
      admits a relative reference beside an IRI, so its default is an
      IRI-reference; withdrawing it requires a scheme. `allow_fragment` admits a
      fragment; withdrawing it refuses one. An IRI's characters beyond US-ASCII
      are RFC 3987's `ucschar`, and `iprivate` within the query, and an IRI is
      judged through the URI it maps to (RFC 3987 §3.1). `uri_type` is the
      US-ASCII family with the same facets.

      An IRI is its text as written: RFC 3986 says which of its parts compare
      without case, and a form over the whole text would fold a path or a query
      that is case-sensitive.
      """
    iri_type => meta.text_type & meta.atom_specification & {
      spec?:           = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3987"
      schemes?:        meta.scheme_set
      allow_relative?: meta.boolean ~ true
      allow_fragment?: meta.boolean ~ true
      normalization?:  = NONE
    }

    @meta.doc:"The schemes an iri_type or uri_type admits."
    scheme_set => !meta.set_type { element_type: meta.scheme_name  min_items: 1 }

    @meta.doc:"""
      A URI scheme (RFC 3986 §3.1): a letter, then letters, digits, "+", "-" and
      ".". A scheme compares without case, so its value is the text folded.
      """
    scheme_name => !meta.identifier_type { start: ASCII  continue: ASCII
      continue_add: "+-."  exclude: "_"  normalization: ASCII_CASEFOLD }

    @meta.doc:"An IRI (RFC 3987 §2.2): the scheme is required."
    iri => !meta.iri_type { allow_relative: false }

    @meta.doc:"""
      Regex constraint vocabulary. Same shape pattern as iri_type, pinned to
      I-Regexp. A regex is its text as written: putting it into another form
      would change what it matches.
      """
    regex_type => meta.text_type & meta.atom_specification & {
      spec?:          = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9485"
      normalization?: = NONE
    }

    regex => !meta.regex_type {}

    @meta.doc:"A type's name: a schema-map key, and a segment of a reference."
    type_name  => meta.identifier

    @meta.doc:"A field's name, in a record or a field group."
    field_name => meta.identifier

    @meta.doc:"A template parameter's name."
    param_name => meta.identifier

    @meta.doc:"How a product's parts are reached: by position, or by name."
    product_access_type => !meta.enum [INDEX NAMED]

    @meta.doc:"Whether a product's number of parts is fixed by its type."
    product_size_type   => !meta.enum [FIXED VARIABLE]

    @meta.doc:"""
      What a record field's `value` is for: FREE has none, DEFAULT is injected
      when the field is omitted and may be overridden, and FIXED is the only
      value the field admits.
      """
    field_role          => !meta.enum [FREE DEFAULT FIXED]

    @meta.doc:"""
      How a record may be realised ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.2). OPEN is the ordinary
      case and the default: the record has instances of its own and may be
      composed onto or refined. ABSTRACT has no instances of its own, so a value
      at a position typed by it is a value of some subtype. FINAL has instances
      and admits no subtype: no composition or refinement may name it as a
      source. Subtraction stays admissible against a FINAL record — a subtracted
      record's chain is empty, so it mints no IS-A edge, which is the only thing
      FINAL constrains.

      **How a subtype is selected is no part of this enum.** An ABSTRACT record
      naming no `discriminators` is selected by the tag; one naming them is
      selected by reading those fields.

      Extensibility is never inherited. A subtype of an ABSTRACT record is OPEN
      unless it says otherwise.

      **A field written `=?` implies ABSTRACT.** Writing `abstract` beside a
      selector asserts what the body already says and is admitted; `final`
      beside one is refused.

      **A family is open across schemas.** [TSON-SCHEMA] §3.3.4 makes `subtypes`
      open, so an importing schema may declare a further member. A host
      language's own closed-set construct, generated from a family, is therefore
      relative to one closure and not a promise the schema makes.

      The member is written as a word at the declaration —
      `pet => abstract { … }`, [TSON-SCHEMA] §12.1 — and OPEN is the absence of
      one.
      """
    record_extension_type => !meta.enum [ABSTRACT FINAL OPEN]

    @meta.doc:"""
      The annotation type marker: a type carrying it is meant as annotation
      metadata ([TSON-SCHEMA] §6).
      """
    annotation => meta.void

    @meta.doc:"""
      Documentation for a declaration, a field or a document. The text is
      CommonMark 0.31.2 (https://spec.commonmark.org/0.31.2/) with no
      extensions. Every string is valid CommonMark, so this refuses nothing and
      says only how the text is displayed. Raw HTML in it is never executed, and
      a renderer need not render it.
      """
    doc => @meta.annotation meta.text

    @meta.doc:"""
      Resolver-attached and derived: discarded and recomputed on ingest
      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §6, §8.1), and marks a resolver-materialised synthetic
      entry at its schema-map key (§8.2).
      """
    synthetic => @meta.annotation meta.void

    @meta.doc:"""
      Resolver-attached and derived: on an entry's schema-map key, the
      application the entry is the instantiation of ([TSON-SCHEMA] §8.2). An
      entry of any kind can be one, so it is a fact about the entry and not
      about a type: the resolver writes it wherever an entry closes an
      application — a record template's member, an instance closed from a
      templated construction, the alias a template's application closes to. On
      ingest it is read as stated, unlike `synthetic`, which is recomputed: it
      is the one fact the entry's value does not state, and what lets an
      imported schema's instantiations unify with a later schema's. A
      declaration never writes it, as it never writes `chain`. Its value is the
      application as the reference written, nested arguments unreduced,
      `@meta.application:foo<bar<text>>`.
      """
    application => @meta.annotation meta.type_kind

    @meta.doc:"""
      Supporting records. A record field is the four facts a reader consults:
      `optional`, that the key may be missing; `voidable`, that the value may be
      void, the void sentinel `_` written in its place; `role`, what `value` is
      for; and `value`, a concrete default or fixed value read against the
      field's declared type — for a `type_kind` slot, a type name. Its name is
      not among them: a field is a value of `record.fields`, keyed by its name.
      Each fact is one mark of the spelling `name?: type? ~ value`: the name's
      `?`, the type's `?`, and `~` or `=` with its value. What omission yields
      is derived, never stored: a missing field that is not optional is an
      error, and a missing optional one with a value has it injected — except at
      a field group's member, whose presence is the group's and which is never
      supplied.

      In a record template it also carries a parameter, in `type` or in `value`,
      and needs no label to do so: a token there is a parameter exactly when its
      text resolves into the enclosing record's `parameters` ([TSON-SCHEMA]
      §5.10's shadowing rule), in resolved output as in source.

      A parametric modifier takes the facts its literal spelling takes
      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.7): `w?: T ~ P` is an optional DEFAULT field and
      `w?: T = P` an optional FIXED one, `w: T = P` is a FIXED field that is not
      optional, and `w: T ~ P` is refused. The parameter stands in `value` until
      an application substitutes its argument there, which changes no other
      fact.

      Which fields a member-dispatched family dispatches on is the enclosing
      record's own statement, `record.discriminators`, and no field says it of
      itself. A field it names is required and not voidable, no member of a
      field group, typed by an atom or an enum, and carries no value of its own;
      each subtype restates it FIXED, the pins pairwise distinct under the field
      type's own equality. In a record template a field FIXED to a parameter is
      named too: each application supplies its pin, and the base reads the field
      with that pin dropped.

      The two facts are the series' two kinds of nothing, and each has one name
      wherever it applies: a slot that may be missing is `optional`, on a record
      field and a field group; a slot whose value may be void is `voidable`, on
      a record field, an array's element, a map's value and a tuple position. A
      container's part is never missing, so a container has no `optional`.
      """
    record_field => {
      type:      meta.type_kind
      optional?: meta.boolean ~ false
      voidable?: meta.boolean ~ false
      role?:     meta.field_role ~ FREE
      value?:    meta.value
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      One position of a tuple: its type, and whether its value may be void. A
      position is never missing, so `[A, B?]` admits `[a _]` and refuses `[a]`.
      """
    tuple_element => {
      element_type: meta.type_kind
      voidable?:    meta.boolean ~ false
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      Field group — a presence rule over fields of a record ([TSON-SCHEMA]
      §5.11). `members` lists the group's options in source order, each the
      fields it holds; a group has at least one option, and an option at least
      one field. Every member is declared in the enclosing record's `fields`,
      `optional` there and carrying no value, and sits in one option of one
      group. An option is chosen when any of its members is present.

      `optional_members` names the members that may be missing once their option
      is chosen, and never the only member of an option; a chosen option holds
      every member it does not name. `optional` says whether the group may be
      missing as a whole, as an optional field's key may: without it exactly one
      option is chosen, with it at most one. A group of one option is either not
      optional with every member named in `optional_members`, so at least one is
      present, or optional with at least two members and one not named. The
      flattened fields-plus-groups encoding is canonical.
      """
    field_group => {
      members:           [[meta.text; 1..]; 1..]
      optional_members?: [meta.text; 1..]
      optional?:         meta.boolean ~ false
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      The fields of a record ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.2): each field's name mapped to
      its facts, in declaration order, which is part of the value. A name is
      written once, by its spelling, as the key; two spellings that are one
      value of the record's `name_type` are a resolver error. The key family is
      the host record's: its `name_type`, so `field_map` states no `key_type`,
      and the family is `field_name` where neither the record nor a supertype
      states one.
      """
    field_map => !meta.namespace { value_type: meta.record_field  ordered: true }

    @meta.doc:"""
      A record: named fields, fixed by the type, with the field groups over
      them.

      `parameters`, where present, make the record a template ([TSON-SCHEMA]
      §5.10) and a **family base**: a type a position may name, ABSTRACT by
      construction, a value there being a value of one of its declared members,
      each IS-A it. A parameter stands only in a field's `type`, a field's
      `value`, or an argument of an application in either or in a parent; the
      rest of the record is what every application shares. Its type and bound
      are derived from where it stands, as `template_param` says.

      `extension` states how the record may be realised, and is OPEN unless the
      record says otherwise; on a record with `parameters` it states how each
      application is realised, the base's own being ABSTRACT whatever it says,
      so `result => abstract <T> { ... }` makes `result<text>` abstract over its
      own members. Of the constructors, only `record` carries it.

      Its chain is `type`'s. On a record with `parameters` a parent may still be
      open — `<T> result<T> & { ... }` — and it is substituted and closed with
      every other reference the record holds, so `ok<text>` reaches an IS-A edge
      to `result<text>` and not to `result<int32>`. `fields` is flattened: a
      composition's fields are its operands' and its own, merged, so a consumer
      that reads values against it never repeats the merge.

      `discriminators` names the fields this record's members are selected by,
      in the order their pins are compared as a tuple, and its presence is what
      makes the family member-dispatched rather than tag-dispatched. Every name
      must name a field the enclosing record declares, and the record must be
      ABSTRACT. The author writes `=?` on the field, which is what lowers into
      this list ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.2).

      `name_type` names the family this record's field names belong to, as an
      enum_type's names its members': every name in `fields`, `discriminators`
      and the groups is a value of it, and so is every field name a data value
      of this record writes. It names an identifier family, a member of
      `identifier_kind`, so every field name is a name under its profile, and
      the field name slots are therefore `text`, each judged by this type. A
      record's name type is set where it is declared and a subtype cannot change
      it: absent, it is its supertypes' one name type, and `field_name`, the
      kernel's identifier, for a record with none; stated beside supertypes, it
      is theirs. Supertypes with different name types do not compose.
      """
    record => meta.product & {
      access_pattern?: meta.product_access_type = NAMED
      size_type?:      meta.product_size_type = FIXED
      fields:          meta.field_map
      groups?:         [meta.field_group]
      extension?:      meta.record_extension_type ~ OPEN
      discriminators?: [meta.text; 1..]
      name_type?:      meta.identifier_kind
      parameters?:     [meta.template_param]
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      A sequence of elements of one type. The container constructors carry no
      parameters: a type slot (array's element_type, map's key_type and
      value_type) is an ordinary required field typed `type_kind`, filled by the
      construction or by a sugar form's desugaring like any other required
      field, and a closed application carries the bound reference as the field's
      value (!array { element_type: person }), its binding record being ordinary
      data of this vocabulary. Inside a held template body ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.10)
      any slot — type, scalar, or collection — may hold a parameter token,
      substituted away before the body is read against this vocabulary.
      `voidable` says whether an element may be void, spelled `[T?]`.

      `ordered` says whether order is part of a container's value: true for an
      array unless it says otherwise, false for a set. It never changes what a
      document may write, and output keeps the order written either way
      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §7.5) — the facet says whether two values differing only in
      order are one value.
      """
    array => meta.product & {
      access_pattern?: meta.product_access_type = INDEX
      size_type?:      meta.product_size_type = VARIABLE
      element_type:    meta.type_kind
      voidable?:       meta.boolean ~ false
      ordered?:        meta.boolean ~ true
      unique_items?:   meta.boolean ~ false
      min_items?:      meta.non_negative_integer
      max_items?:      meta.non_negative_integer
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      The unordered, unique refinement of array. Its bounds are array's: the
      empty set is a set, and a set that must be non-empty says `min_items: 1`.

      The template an author writes is `set`,
      `set => <T> !meta.set_type { element_type: T }`, so a field reads
      `tags: set<text>` where core.tn names it at its root.
      """
    set_type => meta.array ^ {
      voidable?:     = false
      ordered?:      = false
      unique_items?: = true
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      Entries keyed by distinct values of one type. `voidable` says whether an
      entry's value may be void, spelled `{K => V?}`, as array's says it of an
      element; a key is never void.

      `ordered` is false unless the map says otherwise, as for a JSON object:
      two maps holding the same entries in different orders are one value. An
      ordered map, `!meta.map { … ordered: true }`, is one whose entry order is
      part of its value, so those two are distinct, and a host binds it to a map
      that keeps its order. Either way output keeps the order written
      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §7.5).
      """
    map => meta.product & {
      access_pattern?: meta.product_access_type = NAMED
      size_type?:      meta.product_size_type = VARIABLE
      key_type:        meta.type_kind
      value_type:      meta.type_kind
      voidable?:       meta.boolean ~ false
      ordered?:        meta.boolean ~ false
      min_items?:      meta.non_negative_integer
      max_items?:      meta.non_negative_integer
    }

    @meta.doc:"A fixed sequence of positions, each with a type of its own."
    tuple => meta.product & {
      access_pattern?: meta.product_access_type = INDEX
      size_type?:      meta.product_size_type = FIXED
      elements:        [meta.tuple_element]
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      The members of an enum_type. The set's own uniqueness compares the text as
      written; each member is then read as a value of the enum's `name_type`,
      and two that are one value under that type's equality are a duplicate.
      """
    enum_set => !meta.set_type { element_type: meta.text  min_items: 1 }

    @meta.doc:"""
      An enumeration: a closed set of names, and the type they are drawn from
      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §7.4), as a record's `name_type` is its field names'.
      `name_type` names an identifier family — a member of `identifier_kind`, a
      type built with identifier_type — and each member must be a value of it,
      distinct from the others under its equality, and is a name under its
      profile ([TSON-DATA] §8.2). A closed set of text that is no name is the
      text family's own `members` facet, `!text ^ { members: [...] }`; an
      enumeration of names a profile admits beyond the kernel's identifier, such
      as words separated by single spaces, is an enum_type over the author's own
      identifier_type.

      A value `name_type` takes from a constructor resolves where that
      constructor is declared; one an author writes resolves in the author's own
      namespace, so a schema may enumerate names of a naming vocabulary it
      declares itself. `name_type` is fixed once an enum is constructed: a
      refinement narrows the members and never the type.
      """
    enum_type => meta.atom & {
      name_type: meta.identifier_kind
      members:   meta.enum_set
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      An enumeration of identifiers: the positional `!meta.enum [OPEN DONE]`.
      """
    enum => meta.enum_type ^ { name_type?: = meta.identifier }

    @meta.doc:"""
      Closed sum. `disjoint` is a resolver-derived index over the variant list,
      parallel to `subtypes`: true or false by discrimination-class distinctness
      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.4). Declarations never set it; on ingest it MUST be
      discarded and recomputed. It carries the encoding-independent fact each
      encoding's discrimination rules consume ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.4, §7.2).
      """
    choice => meta.sum & {
      variants:  [meta.type_kind]
      disjoint?: meta.boolean
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      Constructor for the open sum over the governing closure: the value names
      its own type by its tag, among the types of the closure ([TSON-SCHEMA]
      §2.2.3), and is validated in full against it. A value naming no type is
      invalid. Where `choice` is the closed sum, enumerating its variants, this
      is the open one, drawing them from the closure; `disjoint` is absent, as
      on every non-choice sum.
      """
    declared_type => meta.sum & {}

    @meta.doc:"""
      A value of a type the closure declares, chosen by the value: the tag names
      the type, the name resolves in the closure exactly as it does anywhere
      else, and the value is validated in full against it. What a schema map's
      entries are.
      """
    declared => !meta.declared_type {}

    @meta.doc:"""
      A name held in a slot: an atom whose values are names, each naming an
      entry whose value's type, past the entry's alias hops ([TSON-SCHEMA]
      §8.3), is `of` or IS-A it. Every entry is a name and a value, and the
      value has a type, its tag: `order => { ... }` holds a value of `record`,
      `int32 => !integer ^ { ... }` a value of `integer_type`, and
      `orders => !api { ... }` a value of `api`. A field's type position, a
      `kind<meta.record>` slot and a `ref<method>` slot all admit a name by that
      one predicate, and differ only in `of`.

      `of` names any type or constructor of the writer's namespace
      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §3.3.1), never a value or an open entry. Where it is a
      constructor, `top` or IS-A it, the values it admits are types, and a slot
      holds a type's name, which takes part in structural identity
      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §8.2). A record written in braces is built with `record`,
      and so is a record template, `top` itself, and a constructor whose
      declaration is a record, so `kind<meta.record>` holds all of them; a
      template over another constructor is no type, and an application of one is
      the type it expands to. Where `of` is any other type, the values it admits
      are values, and a slot holds a value's qualified name: the first segment
      resolves in the writer's namespace and names an entry, and each further
      segment selects a key of a value of a named product, a namespace value's
      key or a record value's field, the record being a namespace whose keys its
      type fixes. A map that is no namespace is not entered.

      A type's name is its entry's path from the document's root: `order`, or
      `geo.address` for a member of a schema map nested as
      `geo => !schema { ... }`, and every name resolves from the root.

      A value is the name, never a value of the type it names. In a data
      document it resolves in the namespace of the governing schema and is
      judged there. In a schema document its grammar is read where it is
      written, and the name is judged when that schema links: the set of names
      a reference admits is the closure's, derived and never written.

      A value may carry an argument list, `geo.address<T>`: after its qualified
      name, an adjacent `<`, one or more arguments, and `>`. It is data grammar
      ([TSON-DATA] §7.4), written unquoted wherever a value stands. An argument
      is a reference or a token, positional against the head's parameters, and
      typed where it lands: a type argument is judged as the parameter's `bound`
      says, and a value argument is a token of the parameter's type
      ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.10). The canonical text has no whitespace but one space
      between arguments: `pair<text integer>`, `maxint<10>`. Identity is the
      parsed form ([TSON-SCHEMA] §8.2), so whitespace inside an argument list is
      free. Every application denotes an entry: a closed one lifts to the entry
      it denotes and is named bare where it stands ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.3), so a
      value carries arguments only where an application is still open, in a
      record template's `parents` and field types, in held text, and in the
      `application` annotation.
      """
    reference => meta.atom & {
      of: meta.type_kind
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      The spelling of a reference whose `of` is a constructor:
      `kind<meta.uuid_type>` is `!meta.reference { of: meta.uuid_type }`, the
      names of the types built with it.
      """
    kind => <T> !meta.reference { of: T }

    @meta.doc:"The names of the identifier families, which a `name_type` names."
    identifier_kind => !meta.reference { of: meta.identifier_type }

    @meta.doc:"The names of the atoms."
    atom_kind => !meta.reference { of: meta.atom }

    @meta.doc:"The names of the types a value can have."
    type_kind => !meta.reference { of: meta.type }

    @meta.doc:"""
      The spelling of a reference to a value: `ref<method>` is
      `!meta.reference { of: method }`, one reference with `kind<method>`.
      """
    ref => <T> !meta.reference { of: T }

    @meta.doc:"""
      Resolver output: a schema map, from each name to the value its entry
      resolves to, a type or a value, each naming its type by its tag. Resolved
      output and source are one grammar — output is source with sugar expanded,
      bodies flattened and chains computed, and an entry written as a
      constructor application resolves to itself. A value of `schema` nested as
      an entry, `geo => !schema { ... }`, declares as the root does: its members
      are entries of the document, each named by its path from the root,
      `geo.address`.
      """
    schema => !meta.namespace { key_type: meta.type_name  value_type: meta.declared }

    @meta.doc:"""
      The set spelling an author writes: `set<text>` where the bare construction
      is `!meta.set_type { element_type: text }`. A §5.10 template, not a
      constructor: it composes with nothing and so is not IS-A `top`.
      """
    set => <T> !meta.set_type { element_type: T }

    @meta.doc:"""
      The base encodings of RFC 4648 that a text encoding may spell a `bytes`
      value in (HEX is §8's base16).
      """
    bytes_encoding => !meta.enum [BASE64 BASE64URL BASE32 HEX]

    @meta.doc:"""
      Octet-sequence constraint vocabulary. Instance is `bytes` in core. The
      value is the octets: equality, identity, content addressing and the length
      facets are all over octets, never over a spelling — `length: 32` is a
      32-byte digest whether it arrives as 64 hex characters, 44 base64
      characters, or 32 raw bytes — so a round trip through any encoding
      preserves the value.

      `encoding` is a selector ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.7): it picks which RFC 4648
      alphabet the text class of encodings — TSON text, JSON, and any encoding
      whose values are character sequences — spells the octets in, and an
      encoding whose values are octets ignores it. A selector picks a spelling,
      so it never changes what two values compare as. It defaults to BASE64 (§4,
      padded), so a `bytes` position is base64 in every text encoding.

      `encoding` is not refinable. Another alphabet is another type, declared as
      its own instance:
      `hexdigest => !meta.bytes_type { encoding: HEX  length: 4 }`. Refining for
      length inherits the alphabet: `sha256 => !bytes ^ { length: 32 }` is a
      base64 sha256.
      """
    bytes_type => meta.atom & {
      encoding?:   meta.bytes_encoding ~ BASE64
      length?:     meta.non_negative_integer
      min_length?: meta.non_negative_integer
      max_length?: meta.non_negative_integer
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      IEEE 754-2019 interchange formats, both radices. Selects the representable
      grid of an approximate (`float_type`) value. The binary formats are the
      familiar base-2 floats; the decimal formats (decimal32/64/128) are base-10
      *floating-point* — still approximate, with their own special values — and
      are NOT the exact fixed-point decimals of `decimal_type`.
      """
    ieee_format => !meta.enum [
      BINARY16 BINARY32 BINARY64 BINARY128 BINARY256
      DECIMAL32 DECIMAL64 DECIMAL128
    ]

    @meta.doc:"""
      Approximate-numeric constraint vocabulary (SQL's approximate tier; ISO/IEC
      11404 approximate real). Instances in core are `float32` and `float64`. An
      approximate value is not stored as written: the receiver rounds it onto
      the discrete IEEE 754-2019 grid named by `format`, so precision may be
      lost and equality comparison is unsafe (ordering is PARTIAL — NaN is
      unordered). The `format` radix (binary or decimal) is the ISO 11404 radix
      characteristic of the type. The value set is that grid together with
      signed zeros, subnormals, the two infinities, and the canonical quiet NaN
      ([TSON-DATA] §5.6); `allow_nan`, `allow_infinity`, `allow_subnormal`, and
      `allow_negative_zero` default to true (`~ true` — the format's full set)
      and may be narrowed to false to exclude those representation artifacts.
      The bounds are optional domain restrictions validated against the value as
      written, before rounding; they do not bound the special values. Not a
      narrowing of the exact tier: infinities and NaN are not exact numbers.

      Bounds follow the header's field-group rule. There is no `multiple_of`;
      use the exact tier (`decimal_type`) when a step is required. A text-class
      token that does not land on the grid is rounded to nearest, ties to even
      (IEEE 754-2019 roundTiesToEven), the default across the format; loss of
      precision is expected, not an error. An encoding that carries the format's
      bits delivers a grid value directly and rounds nothing.
      """
    float_type => meta.atom & meta.atom_specification & {
      spec?:                = "https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/8766229"
      format:               meta.ieee_format
      ( min: meta.value | exclusive_min: meta.value )?
      ( max: meta.value | exclusive_max: meta.value )?
      allow_nan?:           meta.boolean ~ true
      allow_infinity?:      meta.boolean ~ true
      allow_subnormal?:     meta.boolean ~ true
      allow_negative_zero?: meta.boolean ~ true
    }

    @meta.doc:"The members of a decimal_type."
    decimal_member_set => !meta.set_type { element_type: meta.value  min_items: 1 }

    @meta.doc:"""
      Exact-numeric constraint vocabulary (SQL's exact tier; ISO/IEC 11404
      `scaled`, radix 10). Instance in core is `number` — the unrestricted exact
      number, which is also the JSON Schema `number` mapping (arbitrary
      magnitude and precision, finite, no special values). Nothing is rounded on
      the way in, so equality is safe and ordering is TOTAL. Scale is not part
      of the value — `1`, `1.0` and `1.00` are one value, and whether a
      spelling's trailing zeros survive a round trip is an encoding's promise,
      not the type's. `total_digits`/`fraction_digits` are the ISO `scaled`
      precision and scale (SQL `DECIMAL(precision, scale)`): total significant
      digits and digits after the point.

      Bounds follow the header's field-group rule. `multiple_of` is exact here
      (e.g. `multiple_of` 0.05 admits only nickel steps). `fraction_digits` and
      `multiple_of` overlap but are not equivalent: `fraction_digits` 2 admits
      any hundredth, `multiple_of` 0.05 only multiples of a nickel.

      `members` enumerates the admitted values. The resolver reads each member
      under the constrained atom before the set is formed, so `1` and `1.0` are
      one member and a duplicate rather than two, and a member that does not
      parse as a decimal fails at schema load. The header's uniform `members`
      rule applies.
      """
    decimal_type => meta.atom & {
      ( min: meta.value | exclusive_min: meta.value )?
      ( max: meta.value | exclusive_max: meta.value )?
      multiple_of?:     meta.value
      total_digits?:    meta.non_negative_integer
      fraction_digits?: meta.non_negative_integer
      members?:         meta.decimal_member_set
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      Rational constraint vocabulary. Instance is `rational` in core. Exact (ℚ;
      ISO/IEC 11404 `rational`), so bounds and `multiple_of` are exact. Bounds
      follow the header's field-group rule. Constraints operate on the value,
      not the written token: tokens are not normalized, so `"2/4"` and `"1/2"`
      satisfy the same constraints.
      """
    rational_type => meta.atom & {
      ( min: meta.value | exclusive_min: meta.value )?
      ( max: meta.value | exclusive_max: meta.value )?
      multiple_of?: meta.value
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      Allowed component types for a complex value. A closed vocabulary of
      numeric types (naming their core counterparts): exact `INTEGER` (Gaussian
      integers ℤ+ℤi), `NUMBER` (exact arbitrary precision), `RATIONAL` (ℚ+ℚi);
      approximate `FLOAT32`, `FLOAT64`.

      The members are a partial order, not a chain, and a refinement may move
      `component` only to a member whose value set is a subset of the source's:
      `INTEGER ⊂ NUMBER ⊂ RATIONAL` are the exact tiers and `FLOAT32 ⊂ FLOAT64`
      the approximate ones, and the two families are incomparable. So
      `!complex ^ { component: INTEGER }` narrows and its IS-A holds, while
      `!complex ^ { component: FLOAT64 }` is refused: a float64 complex is not a
      `complex`, and is declared as its own type,
      `!meta.complex_type { component: FLOAT64 }`.
      """
    complex_component => !meta.enum [INTEGER NUMBER RATIONAL FLOAT32 FLOAT64]

    @meta.doc:"""
      Complex constraint vocabulary. Instance is `complex` in core. A complex
      value is a pair of components (real and imaginary parts). `component`
      selects the type of both parts from the closed `complex_component`
      vocabulary; it defaults to `NUMBER` (a DEFAULT, §5.2), so a bare complex
      has exact components — a defaulted selector. Complex has no useful total
      order (`@ordering:NONE`), so the order-based facets of the other numeric
      tiers do not apply here. Exactness is inherited from `component` rather
      than fixed: `INTEGER`/`NUMBER`/`RATIONAL` components give an exact
      complex, `FLOAT32`/`FLOAT64` an approximate one, so `complex` carries no
      `@exact` marker of its own.
      """
    complex_type => meta.atom & {
      component?: meta.complex_component ~ NUMBER
    }

    @meta.doc:"Date constraint vocabulary. Instance is `date` in core."
    date_type => meta.atom & meta.atom_specification & {
      spec?: = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3339"
      ( min: meta.value | exclusive_min: meta.value )?
      ( max: meta.value | exclusive_max: meta.value )?
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      Time constraint vocabulary. Instance is `time` in core. `precision` is a
      constraint on the value, not on a spelling: `precision: N` admits a value
      that is a whole number of 10^-N seconds, so `precision: 3` is millisecond
      resolution and `precision: 0` whole seconds. The atom is exact — nothing
      is truncated; a value off the grid is rejected ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.5).
      Reading admits any spelling of an admitted value, trailing zeros included,
      so `12:00:00.500` is admitted under `precision: 1`; a text encoding
      writing the value writes at most N fractional digits, `12:00:00.5`.
      """
    time_type => meta.atom & meta.atom_specification & {
      spec?:      = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3339"
      ( min: meta.value | exclusive_min: meta.value )?
      ( max: meta.value | exclusive_max: meta.value )?
      precision?: meta.non_negative_integer
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      Datetime constraint vocabulary. Instance is `datetime` in core.
      `precision` as for `time_type`: the value is a whole number of 10^-N
      seconds, a constraint on the value ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.5).
      """
    datetime_type => meta.atom & meta.atom_specification & {
      spec?:      = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3339"
      ( min: meta.value | exclusive_min: meta.value )?
      ( max: meta.value | exclusive_max: meta.value )?
      precision?: meta.non_negative_integer
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      Elapsed-time constraint vocabulary. Instance is `duration` in core.
      Lexical form is the RFC 3339 Appendix A `duration` production with three
      extensions and one restriction: an optional leading `-`; a fraction on the
      seconds component only, written with `.` (`PT0.5S`, never `PT1.5H` or
      `PT0,5S`); any subset of D, H, M, S may be omitted provided at least one
      component is present and order is kept (`PT1H30S` is admitted); and the Y
      and month-M components are not admitted — `P1M` is one month and belongs
      to `period`; a minute is `PT1M`. `PnW` stands alone, as in the ABNF
      (`P1W2D` is an error). A day is exactly 86400 s and a week 7 days.

      The value is a signed exact decimal number of seconds — `number`'s value
      space, in seconds: nothing is rounded. So `PT90M`, `PT1H30M` and
      `P0DT5400S` are one value, `PT0S`, `P0D` and `-PT0S` are one value, and
      ordering is TOTAL. Bounds compare the value, not the token.

      Both ends of that space are fixed at a signed 64-bit count of nanoseconds.
      The seconds component admits at most nine fractional digits, and a
      magnitude may not exceed 2^63 - 1 nanoseconds (about 292 years) — stated
      as a magnitude, so negating an admitted duration always yields an admitted
      one. A processor MUST represent every value in that range and MUST reject
      one outside it whether or not its own representation could hold it.

      `precision` as for `time_type`, and it is `fraction_digits` on the seconds
      count: the value is a whole number of 10^-N seconds, so `precision: 0`
      admits whole seconds only and `precision: 9` is the nanosecond grid. A
      constraint on the value and not on a spelling — `PT0.50S` is a whole
      number of tenths — and it may not exceed 9. `multiple_of` is `number`'s
      `multiple_of` on that same count, under the header's uniform rule: `PT15M`
      admits only quarter-hour values.
      """
    duration_type => meta.atom & meta.atom_specification & {
      spec?:       = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3339#appendix-A"
      ( min: meta.value | exclusive_min: meta.value )?
      ( max: meta.value | exclusive_max: meta.value )?
      precision?:  meta.non_negative_integer
      multiple_of?: meta.value
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      Calendar-span constraint vocabulary. Instance is `period` in core. Lexical
      form is the RFC 3339 Appendix A `duration` production restricted to an
      optional leading `-`, then `P` followed by a Y component, an M component,
      or both in that order; no fraction, no W or D component and no `T` part
      (`P1M15D`, `P1Y0D` and `P1YT1H` are errors — use `duration`, or a record
      with a `period` field and a `duration` field, for mixed spans). The value
      is a signed integer number of months, so `P1Y` and `P12M` are equal,
      `P0Y`, `P0M` and `-P0M` are equal, and ordering is TOTAL. Bounds compare
      the value, not the token. `multiple_of` is a period under the header's
      uniform rule: `P3M` admits only whole quarters.
      """
    period_type => meta.atom & meta.atom_specification & {
      spec?:       = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3339#appendix-A"
      ( min: meta.value | exclusive_min: meta.value )?
      ( max: meta.value | exclusive_max: meta.value )?
      multiple_of?: meta.value
    }

    @meta.doc:"UUID constraint vocabulary. Instance is `uuid` in core."
    uuid_type => meta.atom & meta.atom_specification & {
      spec?:    = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9562"
      version?: meta.non_negative_integer
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      URI constraint vocabulary per RFC 3986. Instances are `uri` and
      `uri_reference` in core. The facets are iri_type's, over RFC 3986's
      grammar: `schemes` lists the admitted schemes as scheme_name values, which
      fold case; `allow_relative` admits a relative reference (§4.2) beside a
      URI (§3), so its default is a URI-reference (§4.1); `allow_fragment`
      admits a fragment (§3.5), and withdrawing it beside `allow_relative`
      leaves an absolute-URI (§4.3). A URI is US-ASCII (§2), and its text as
      written, as an IRI is.
      """
    uri_type => meta.text_type & meta.atom_specification & {
      spec?:           = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3986"
      schemes?:        meta.scheme_set
      allow_relative?: meta.boolean ~ true
      allow_fragment?: meta.boolean ~ true
      normalization?:  = NONE
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      Email constraint vocabulary. Instance is `email` in core. An address is
      its text as written: a mailbox's local part is case-sensitive (RFC 5321
      §2.4), so a form over the whole text could merge two mailboxes.
      """
    email_type => meta.text_type & meta.atom_specification & {
      spec?:          = "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5322"
      normalization?: = NONE
    }

    @meta.doc:"""
      Ordering classification (ISO/IEC 11404 / XSD fundamental facet): whether
      the value space has an order relation. Not a container's `ordered` facet,
      which says whether element order is part of a value.
      """
    ordering => @meta.annotation !meta.enum [NONE PARTIAL TOTAL]

    @meta.doc:"""
      Boundedness classification (ISO/IEC 11404 / XSD fundamental facet): true
      when the value space has a finite least and greatest value.
      """
    bounded => @meta.annotation meta.boolean

    @meta.doc:"""
      Exactness classification (ISO/IEC 11404 characterizing property): true for
      exact types (values preserved as written), false for approximate types
      (values rounded to a representation grid).
      """
    exact => @meta.annotation meta.boolean

    @meta.doc:"Numeric type marker annotation."
    numeric => @meta.annotation meta.void

    @meta.doc:"""
      Author's assertion that a choice's variants are distinguishable by an
      encoding's form resolution — discrimination-class distinctness, not
      disjointness of inhabitance — checked against the resolver's derived
      `disjoint` fact in the `!meta.choice` body ([TSON-SCHEMA] §5.4, §8.1). The
      fact is class-stable: a variant whose forms straddle classes (an
      approximate atom still admitting NaN or the infinities, a map with a
      compound key) has no class, so the untagged values of a choice are one set
      in every encoding. A void-targeted presence marker like `@numeric`:
      written bare, and presence is the assertion. Carries no decode force — the
      resolver computes disjointness whether or not the annotation is present.
      The derivation is total: present and refuted is a schema-load error;
      present and verified, or absent, is silent.
      """
    disjoint => @meta.annotation meta.void

    @meta.doc:"""
      Marks a definition or field as deprecated. A bare presence marker like
      `@numeric`; the reason, where there is one, is `@doc`'s to give.
      """
    deprecated => @meta.annotation meta.void

    @meta.doc:"Short human-readable name for a definition or field."
    title => @meta.annotation meta.text

    @meta.doc:"""
      A note for a schema's maintainers rather than its readers (JSON Schema's
      `$comment`): where `@doc` is what a documentation tool shows, `@comment`
      is what it leaves out.
      """
    comment => @meta.annotation meta.text

    @meta.doc:"""
      Example values for a definition or field, each the text a reader is shown:
      conventionally the value written in TSON notation, so a record's example
      is a string such as `"{ x: 1 }"`. Nothing parses or checks an example
      against the type it illustrates; the annotation is documentation only.
      """
    examples => @meta.annotation [meta.text]

    @meta.doc:"""
      Marks a field an encoding's consumers may read but not write. A bare
      presence marker like `@numeric`: absence is the default, so there is no
      false to write (JSON Schema's `readOnly: false` maps to no annotation).
      `@read_only` and `@write_only` on one field is a schema-load error.
      """
    read_only => @meta.annotation meta.void

    @meta.doc:"""
      Marks a field an encoding's consumers may write but not read. A bare
      presence marker, on `@read_only`'s terms.
      """
    write_only => @meta.annotation meta.void  }
}
